What this all means:
This capture shows TCP segments carrying encrypted TLS Application Data over port 443 using IPv6. The packet contains 1370 bytes of encrypted payload, and Wireshark indicates that this segment is part of a larger TLS record that has been reassembled across multiple TCP packets. Because TLS encrypts all application-layer content after the handshake, the payload appears as random bytes in the hex view. This capture demonstrates how Wireshark displays encrypted HTTPS traffic at the TCP layer and how TCP reassembly is used to reconstruct full TLS records.
This is exactly how SOC analysts document encrypted traffic.
